-- Rules of Court 2021 (Singapore), G.N. No. S 914/2021.
-- ORDER 38: COMPANIES ACT (lines 5404 to 5510 of the source).  LAYER 1: the law, objectively.
-- Source: a plain-text rendering made by a converter script from Singapore Statutes Online.
-- It is not the official text, and nothing here has been checked against the official text.
--
-- HOW TO READ A RESULT.  Every decision function answers `MAYBE BOOLEAN` unless it says otherwise.
--   JUST TRUE    the Order says yes on these facts.
--   JUST FALSE   the Order says no in its own words (an "if", "where" or "unless" that is not met, a period that has run, a flat negative).
--   NOTHING      the Order does not answer on these facts: a bare "must" that has not been met (the text
--                does not say what follows; O 3 r 2(4), outside this range, lists what the Court may do),
--                a scope limit, a time the Court fixes and has not fixed, or a term the Act defines.
-- A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.
-- O 3 r 2(1) is a caveat on every answer: the requirements are subject to the Court's discretion to order otherwise.
--
-- PERIODS.  The count of a period is O 3 r 3, in another part.  Every "in time" function takes
-- `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act` (or, for a bar on a date, `o 3 r 3 the first day on which the act may be done`)
-- as a DATE, and a filing or service date as a plain DATE.  A period counted BACKWARDS from a hearing ("at least 28 days
-- before the date fixed for the hearing", r 14) is given to the function the same way: the caller computes the last day.
--
-- SEAMS.  Order 9 (r 4(1)) is another part's; r 4(1) is asked as "does it limit Order 9" and answers JUST FALSE.

